{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1M6XPfvha4Q2jTHVzgQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. I did not find a proof I could formalize, so I left `/work/combi_imosl_2015_c6.lean` unchanged.\n\nThe statement is ISL 2015 C6, and the difficult case is an infinite `S`. If `S` is finite, the result is easy: for any `N`, take `m = max N (∑ s ∈ S) + 1`. No subset of `S` sums to `m`, so `m` is not clean. I did not write that case up in Lean.\n\nFor infinite `S`, I tried the generating-function route. With `f(n)` the number of odd-size subsets of `S` summing to `n`, cleanness means `f(n) = 1` for all large `n`, and `∏(1+x^s) − ∏(1−x^s) = 2·F(x)`. I could not turn that into a contradiction. I also tried to find two distinct odd representations of the same large `n` by exchanging elements, and that did not produce one either. Formalizing the official argument would take a substantial development in Lean and Mathlib, which is beyond what I can reliably finish here."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":409,"cache_read_input_tokens":1887,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":409,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e1936ac-9fa3-434f-bd5d-203d3f642782","uuid":"7787b5ff-f542-4d8b-9df8-0765890bcef3","timestamp":"2026-10-14T02:33:41.625Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1M6X555fo6udaybM8aW"}
